{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791024411,"nonce":"JwLN4HYm0qLUqmCvfRM1LoPPShI=","title":"Consider krio-parallel for ash's worker pool, or a parallel-for entry point","message":"Suggested by the ChimpMonk session (at its user's request), 2026-10-03.\n\n**Case.** ChimpMonk's parallel narrowphase splits each physics segment into four slices of ~100-150 us, four rounds per World.step. On ash's FixedThreadPool it is already 10-19% faster than serial in AOT on a quiet Mac (32 3D stacks: 773-834 -\u003e 654-692 ms; 256 3D stacks: 1155 -\u003e 932 ms; 64 2D stacks: ~850 -\u003e ~740 ms), but dispatch is a visible share of each round, and the pool hang 9fac9a5 blocks shipping it. ChimpMonk issue: git-bug 42d4f5bbb1caed50a9c5e283b9173a63e6017640c685b233c0843c3532e1d72a in the chimpmonk repo.\n\n**Native numbers** (Rust, 4 workers, one round = spawn 4 jobs and wait for all), measured by the ChimpMonk session: krio-parallel Cluster with SpinPark 4.5 us per empty round and 5.5 us with 12 us of total work; std channels plus park 12 us and 22 us. ash today, PoolBench (8d19050): ~15-20 us of overhead per round on the NUC, ~30-35 us on the Mac.\n\n**Constraints, as the ChimpMonk session noted them:**\n- Only ash can own the integration. krio's workers would run ash-compiled Haxe callbacks, so they need ash's thread registration, TLAB and JIT state, and must cooperate with stop-the-world safepoints. SpinPark agents spin outside any safepoint.\n- krio-parallel ships no sleeping Park backend yet (its README says each target needs one). A game-friendly backend would spin while a step is in progress and sleep between frames.\n- Its unit of work is `Box\u003cdyn Task + Send\u003e`, so Haxe jobs need a Task adapter that enters the VM on the worker.\n\n**What ash's own measurements say first.** Spinning before sleeping in ash's scheduler (an `ASH_SPIN_US` experiment on the fiber endpoints) made the pool slower on the Mac at every budget: per round of 4 small jobs, off 31-43 us, 20 us 44-53, 50 us 48-59, 100 us 55-70. So ash's per-round overhead is probably not the park/wake itself. Profile where those 30 us go (fiber install/resume, the Deque and Lock natives, GC blocking transitions, the closure call into the job) before choosing a replacement.\n\nSource of the native benchmark: the ChimpMonk session scratchpad krio-bench/ (temporary); krio is at /Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/krio.","files":null}]}